== Summary ==
The deity enshrined at the Inner Shrine of Ise Jingu, Amaterasu Omikami, is considered the ancestral deity of the emperors. Up until the era of the 10th Emperor, Emperor Suinin, it is said that the emperor and Amaterasu Omikami shared the same residence. Initially, she was enshrined within the imperial palace itself, but fearing this arrangement, the emperor entrusted her divine spirit to Princess Toyosukiiri-hime, who established a sacred fence known as the "Iseki-no-himorogi" in Iseshiro, Kasafu village, Yamato province. This began a search for an ideal location for her permanent enshrinement, which led to several relocations. Eventually, the 11th Emperor, Emperor Suijin's fourth princess, Princess Yamatohime-no-mikoto, took over this mission, and after approximately 90 years, the shrine was moved to its current location. The "Iseki-no-himorogi" is first mentioned during the relocation of Amaterasu Omikami to Yamato Kasafu village in the Chronicles of Emperor Suinin, where it is imagined as a facility for the placement of a divine mirror, symbolizing a sacred area akin to a rock seat, with a vast horizontal landscape preserving the tradition of the Iseshiro form. At Ise Jingu, the Shin-no-mihashira, a central sacred pillar, is largely buried in the ground with its surroundings wrapped in sakaki (sacred tree), and ritual pottery symbolizes the rituals. It is said that when the divine mirror of Amaterasu Omikami was first dedicated, a five-hundred-year-old tree from Heavenly Longevity Mountain was dug up, with jewels hung on its upper branches and a mirror on its middle branches, but the sakaki-decorated Shin-no-mihashira symbolizes the appearance of this sacred tree during rituals. The relocation process is not mentioned in the "Kojiki," but is briefly noted in the "Nihon Shoki," more detailed in the "Kotai Jingu Gishiki," and most elaborately in the "Yamatohime-no-mikoto Seiki," a medieval text from the "Shinto Gobusho."

The deity of the Outer Shrine, Toyouke-no-Ōmikami, although not mentioned in the "Kojiki" or "Nihon Shoki," is said in the "Toyuke Gū Gishiki" and "Yamatohime-no-mikoto Seiki" to have been relocated from Tango Province (now part of Tanba Province) to her current site by divine command of Amaterasu Omikami during the era of the 21st Emperor, Emperor Yūryaku.

The tale of Amaterasu Omikami's wanderings is considered similar to the ancestral deity stories in the "Hitachi Province Fudoki" about Mount Tsukuba and the folk tales about Kōbō Daishi. Since many shrine origin stories involve deities traveling to find their enshrinement locations, this is considered a typical motif of a "traveling deity."

The origin tradition of the "Former Ise" is derived from texts like the "Kotai Jingu Gishiki," "Toyuke Gū Gishiki," "Kogo Shūi," and "Engishiki." Although it was previously said to involve around twenty locations, it now includes over sixty. These traditions are based on those transmitted to various shrines, and there are cases where nearby locations claim to be the traditional site, leaving the truth of these claims unclear.

