== Summary ==
The deity enshrined at the Inner Shrine (内宮) of Ise Jingu (伊勢神宮), [[Amaterasu|Amaterasu Omikami]] (天照大御神), is considered the [[Oyagami|ancestral deity]] (皇祖神) of the emperors. Up until the era of the 10th Emperor, [[Emperor Sujin]] (崇神天皇), it is said that the emperor and Amaterasu Omikami shared the same residence, known as "sharing the same bed and palace" (同床共殿). Initially, she was enshrined within the {{ill|Imperial Palace|ja|皇居}} (皇居), but fearing this arrangement, the emperor entrusted her divine spirit to Princess [[User:Immanuelle/Toyosukiiri-hime|Toyosukiiri-hime]] (豊鋤入姫命), who established a sacred fence known as the "Iseki-no-himorogi" (磯城の神籬) in Iseshiro, Kasafu village (笠縫邑), [[Yamato Province|Yamato province]] (倭国). This began a search for an ideal location for her permanent enshrinement, which led to several relocations. Eventually, the 11th Emperor, [[Emperor Suinin|Emperor Suijin]]'s (垂仁天皇) fourth princess, Princess {{ill|Yamatohime-no-mikoto|ja|倭姫命|en}} (倭姫命), took over this mission, and after approximately 90 years, the shrine was moved to its current location. The "Iseki-no-himorogi" is first mentioned during the relocation of Amaterasu Omikami to Yamato Kasafu village in the Chronicles of Emperor Suinin, where it is imagined as a facility for the placement of a [[User:Immanuelle/Shinto mirror|divine mirror]], symbolizing a sacred area akin to a rock seat, with a vast horizontal landscape preserving the tradition of the Iseshiro form. At Ise Jingu, the Shin-no-mihashira (心御柱), a central sacred pillar, is largely buried in the ground with its surroundings wrapped in sakaki (榊), and ritual pottery symbolizes the rituals. It is said that when the divine mirror of Amaterasu Omikami was first dedicated, a five-hundred-year-old tree from {{Ill|Mount Amanokagu|en}} was dug up, with jewels hung on its upper branches and a mirror on its middle branches, but the sakaki-decorated Shin-no-mihashira symbolizes the appearance of this sacred tree during rituals. 